 **GDPR.** With resume on, a conversation outlives its socket, so you own the retention bound: set `maxAgeMs` on `InMemorySessionStore` (eight hours by default), use `MongoSessionStore`'s erasure methods, and see _Control what the engine keeps in server-side conversation history_ (re-cinq/HALEngine#41). An unbounded retained store is a retention breach, not a memory leak.
 
+## Conversation List
+
+A client that wants a history sidebar or a conversation picker sends `{type: 'list_sessions'}` over its open socket and is answered with one `session_list` frame carrying the conversations its own authenticated user holds — `sessionId`, `createdAt`, `updatedAt` (ISO 8601 strings) and `entryCount` per row, most recent activity first. There is no user field on the frame to get wrong: the list is always the connection's, and any `userId` a client sends is dropped. Nothing to enable — the frame is always understood — and a store that implements no `listFor` (see [session stores](session-stores.md#listing-a-users-conversations)) answers an empty list.
+
+A list request is answered beside the heartbeat, not on the turn lock, so it never queues behind an answer that is still streaming. Page with `limit` (50 by default, 200 at most) and `before`, which takes the last row the client saw — both its `updatedAt` and its `sessionId`, so two conversations saved in the same millisecond cannot straddle a page boundary.
+
+**Opening a row.** A client rejoining a listed conversation connects with `?sessionId=<id>`, which is the resume path — its ownership check, its replay (see [Session Resume](#session-resume)). The id is read only when resume is enabled, so a deployment that lists conversations wants `transport.resume` on as well; with it off the list renders and nothing on it can be opened.
+
+**GDPR.** A row is personal data — that a user held a conversation, and when — and no row carries conversation content. What the list reaches, the store holds: the retention bound stays yours, set `maxAgeMs` and the erasure methods on the store, and see _Control what the engine keeps in server-side conversation history_ (re-cinq/HALEngine#41).
+
